-- #1182 ON THE INTERFACE-DECLARATION AXIS.
--
-- This is test/must_fail_fixtures/1182-two-ifaces-same-method-name-order-decides/main.mdk
-- BYTE-FOR-BYTE in its declarations -- same `data Q`, same two interfaces, same two
-- impls, same call -- and the ONLY thing this corpus does differently is WHICH AXIS
-- IS PERMUTED. That pin permutes the two `impl` BLOCKS. This case permutes the two
-- `interface` DECLARATIONS and leaves the impls exactly where they are.
--
-- ⭐ THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS. #1182's fix will re-key
-- impl selection from an interface SPELLING to an interface IDENTITY. A fix that is
-- merely NAME-SCOPED drains the impl-order pin -- and can leave the answer still
-- decided by which interface was DECLARED last. The existing pin cannot tell those
-- two outcomes apart, because it never moves this axis. This case can.
--
-- ⚠️ NOT AN ASSERTION THAT 1 IS RIGHT OR 2 IS RIGHT. Same disclaimer as the pin's:
-- which interface `m` belongs to is genuinely in dispute (#1182 names two viable
-- fixes that disagree about the value). The assertable defect is the ORDER
-- DEPENDENCE -- reordering two interface declarations changes no program meaning.
data Q a b c = Q a b c

interface A1 a where
  m : a -> Int

interface A2 a where
  m : a -> Int

impl A1 (Q Int y z) where
  m _ = 1

impl A2 (Q x Bool z) where
  m _ = 2

main = println (m (Q 1 True 7))
